WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.970
Orders in Council.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.090
Executive power without parliamentary debate.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:13.477
Orders in Council allow the federal cabinet to enact regulations, make appointments, and implement policy decisions without a vote in the House of Commons.

00:00:13.477 --> 00:00:17.511
When used for routine administrative matters, they are unremarkable.

00:00:17.511 --> 00:00:27.102
When used to ban fifteen hundred firearms models, freeze bank accounts, or implement sweeping policy changes, they become a mechanism for bypassing democratic accountability.

00:00:27.102 --> 00:00:33.284
Documented cases of Orders in Council used to bypass parliamentary debate on major policy decisions.

00:00:33.284 --> 00:00:35.954
The May 2020 firearms ban.

00:00:35.954 --> 00:00:38.988
The Emergencies Act economic orders.

00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:41.420
Carbon tax implementation.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:47.386
Each case: executive action on a politically significant issue without a vote in the House of Commons.

00:00:47.386 --> 00:00:49.954
The executive overreach pattern.

00:00:49.954 --> 00:00:54.454
Orders in Council are constitutionally legitimate for routine administration.

00:00:54.454 --> 00:01:00.704
They become democratic deficits when used to implement politically significant policy without parliamentary debate.

00:01:00.704 --> 00:01:04.136
The firearms ban affected millions of legal owners.

00:01:04.136 --> 00:01:07.340
The bank account freezes violated Charter rights.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.897
In both cases, the executive acted without a vote.

00:01:10.897 --> 00:01:16.965
The courts ruled on the Emergencies Act two years later — long after the executive achieved its objective.

00:01:16.965 --> 00:01:20.465
The remedy arrived too late to function as accountability.
